14:50:21 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Running out of IPv4 addresses does **not** mean the Internet shuts down or existing IPv4 devices stop working. It means the organizations that distribute public IPv4 address blocks—regional Internet registries—have little or no unused address space left to hand out under normal policies. This has effectively already happened in most regions. What happens instead: - **Existing IPv4 addresses keep working.** A website, server, or home connection with an IPv4 address does not lose it merely because the pool is exhausted. - **New addresses become harder and more expensive to obtain.** ISPs and companies may buy or lease addresses from organizations that no longer need them, creating an IPv4 transfer market. - **More NAT is used.** Many customers share one public IPv4 address through Carrier-Grade NAT (CGNAT). Your home router already does a smaller version of this: many private devices share one public address. - **Some things become less convenient with shared addresses.** Running inbound servers, port forwarding, certain games, peer-to-peer apps, IP-based reputation systems, and abuse tracing can be harder. A shared address also has a limited number of usable ports. - **IPv6 becomes the long-term solution.** IPv6 has an enormous address space, so networks can give devices globally unique addresses again. During the transition, networks often run both IPv4 and IPv6 (“dual stack”), or use translation/proxy systems so IPv6-only users can still reach IPv4 sites. There are also ways IPv4 space reappears: companies return unused blocks, addresses are recovered from invalid assignments, or blocks are transferred to others. So IPv4 addresses are scarce, not literally gone forever. In short: IPv4 exhaustion mainly turns IPv4 into a constrained, traded resource and pushes the Internet toward NAT and IPv6; it does not cause an Internet-wide outage.
