14:50:59 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That article may well have been right, depending on what it meant by “the Internet’s founders.” The **original designers of IPv4 did not have a detailed plan for IPv4 exhaustion**. IPv4 was designed in the 1970s, when a 32-bit address space—roughly 4.3 billion addresses—looked enormous compared with the number of computers anyone expected to connect. The original Internet was a research network, not a forecast of billions of phones, home devices, cloud servers, sensors, and virtual machines. So there was no original, built-in plan such as: - a scheduled replacement date, - a reserved migration mechanism everyone would be required to use, - a global authority empowered to force upgrades, - or a guarantee that IPv4 and its successor would interoperate seamlessly forever. In that sense, “they never planned for running out” is fair. What happened later was that, by the late 1980s and early 1990s, network engineers recognized the problem and developed a **response plan after the fact**: - conserve addresses with CIDR and tighter allocation policies; - use private addressing and NAT to reduce public-address demand; - create IPv6 as a successor; - expect a gradual transition where both protocols coexist. That was coordinated work, but it was not the same thing as the original Internet having been designed with IPv4 exhaustion handled from the beginning. And the eventual reality was more improvised than the IPv6 transition vision suggested. NAT became far more central and permanent than many protocol designers wanted. IPv4 address transfers and leasing became normal. CGNAT, tunneling, DNS-based tricks, and protocol translation filled gaps. IPv6 deployment proceeded unevenly because IPv4-plus-NAT remained “good enough” for many operators. So the most accurate version is: > IPv4 exhaustion was not originally planned for; it was recognized later, mitigated deliberately, and never fully resolved through the clean global IPv6 migration that engineers hoped would occur. Your recollection is therefore not really contradicted by the later timeline—it distinguishes the **original lack of planning** from the **later, partial and decentralized attempt to cope with the consequences**.
