14:51:29 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes—if by “plan for more IPv4 addresses” you mean **expanding the IPv4 address space itself while remaining IPv4**, then you are right. IPv4 has a fixed 32-bit address field. That gives it a finite namespace of \(2^{32}\), about 4.3 billion possible numerical addresses. There is no standards-compliant way to turn an IPv4 address into, say, a 48- or 64-bit address without changing the packet format, software, routing systems, APIs, and assumptions throughout the network. Once you change that fundamental field, you no longer have IPv4. You have a new Internet Protocol—which is what IPv6 is. The later measures did not create additional globally routable IPv4 addresses: - **CIDR** used the existing IPv4 numbers more efficiently; it reduced waste from the old class-based allocation model. - **Reclaiming or transferring address blocks** redistributes existing IPv4 addresses. - **Private addressing and NAT** let many devices share one public IPv4 address, but do not give those devices distinct public IPv4 addresses. - **CGNAT** repeats that sharing at ISP scale. - **Using ports as an extra identifier** can increase the number of connections or subscribers behind an address, but the public address is still the same 32-bit IPv4 address. There were, of course, discussions and proposals around possible successor protocols in the 1990s—not only the design that became IPv6—but the result was always going to require a protocol transition. There was no magic “IPv4.1” expansion that old IPv4 equipment could transparently understand. So the concise technical statement is: > IPv4 was never designed with an in-protocol address-space expansion path. When its 32-bit namespace became too small, the choices were conserve and share existing addresses, or deploy a different protocol with larger addresses. IPv6 is not “more IPv4”; it is the replacement address family intended to solve the problem IPv4 itself cannot solve.
